// A REBOUND DEADLINE MUST BOUND WHAT A SCOPE WAITS ON, not only what it reads.
//
// `with Run.context = Context.with_timeout(…) { … }` writes the tightened Context into the
// app-field cell, where `Context.deadline_remaining` reads it. Every BLOCKING
// operation has to observe it as well. Eight of them select on the frame's Go
// context: `timer.sleep`, `Sender.send`, `Receiver.receive`, `Task.await`,
// `Task.outcome`, the `concurrent` scope wait, `Supervisor.flush`, and the
// `CancelIfDone` safe point. So the rebound Nomi deadline has to reach that
// Go context too.
//
// Getting this wrong is invisible to most output comparisons, because a
// deadline that is only too LOOSE produces no wrong string: a blocking call
// that consulted the unfloored deadline would simply wait too long.
// `rt.ContextWithFloor` and `rt/context.go` state the same requirement from
// the runtime side.
//
// The shape is the tour's `concurrency.md:L812`, which bounds a five-minute
// sleep with a 50ms deadline and answers in a fraction of a second. The corpus
// carries the shape as well, so the check does not depend on the tour.
//
// A run that ignores the rebound deadline prints no wrong line here either. It
// hangs for the hour `slow` sleeps, and the harness's timeout is what reports
// it; the output rows below then say which scope was waiting and which was
// not.
//
// # THE REAL CLOCK, DELIBERATELY, AND NOT A COUNTER
//
// No `clock Clock.Virtual` and no `tests` group that would put this in a
// `testing/synctest` bubble. Virtual time makes a sleep INSTANT, so this file
// would pass under it whether the deadline is observed or not — the bug
// survives precisely where the sleep is real. The trade is that the file takes
// real milliseconds; the sleeps are sized so a correct implementation finishes
// in well under a second and a broken one takes an hour.
//
// It is also not a counter. Nothing here asserts "cancellation was polled N
// times": the observable is that a bounded wait RESOLVES EARLY and reports
// `Cancelled`, which is what a reader of std/timer is promised
// ("cancellation of that block's context ends the sleep early without
// producing a user-visible error") and what no safe point can deliver, because
// a blocking sleep reaches none of the three.
//
// The unbounded control is what makes the bounded rows non-vacuous: without it
// a build that reported `Cancelled` for every task would pass.
import {
    std/duration.Duration
    std/io
    std/tasks.{Outcome, Task}
    std/timer
}

// The context is read and replaced as an application field, which needs a
// boot to name the application type.
struct Run {
    context: Context
}

fn boot(): Run {
    Run{context: Context.root()}
}

// An hour, so a wait that is not bounded cannot finish inside any timeout.
fn slow(): Int {
    timer.sleep(Duration.hours(1))
    1
}

fn quick(): Int {
    timer.sleep(Duration.milliseconds(1))
    2
}

// The deadline covers `slow` and nothing else. `Task.outcome` sits outside it.
fn slow_bounded(): Int {
    with Run.context = Context.with_timeout(Run.context, Duration.milliseconds(20))
    slow()
}

// A deadline the work fits inside, so the bounded case is not "always
// cancelled". Same construct, opposite answer.
fn quick_bounded(): Int {
    with Run.context = Context.with_timeout(Run.context, Duration.seconds(30))
    quick()
}

fn describe(label: String, outcome: Outcome<Int>): Unit {
    case outcome {
        .Completed(v) -> io.print(label + ": completed " + Display.to_string(v))
        .Cancelled -> io.print(label + ": cancelled")
        .Failed(_) -> io.print(label + ": failed")
    }
}

fn main() {
    // BOUNDED AND EXCEEDED: the sleep must end early and the task unwind.
    bounded = concurrent {
        t = Task.spawn(|| slow_bounded())
        Task.outcome(t)
    }

    describe("bounded", bounded)

    // BOUNDED AND MET: the same construct with room to finish. The control that
    // stops the row above from passing for a build that cancels everything.
    met = concurrent {
        t = Task.spawn(|| quick_bounded())
        Task.outcome(t)
    }

    describe("met", met)

    // NO REBIND AT ALL: a deadline-free scope must still complete, so the frame
    // the rebind installs is shown to be scoped rather than sticky.
    plain = concurrent {
        t = Task.spawn(|| quick())
        Task.outcome(t)
    }

    describe("plain", plain)

    // AFTER the bounded scope has exited, in the SAME activation: the caller's
    // own waits must not have inherited the child's deadline. This is what the
    // frame restore buys, and a build that installed the deadline without
    // restoring it would print `cancelled` here.
    after = concurrent {
        t = Task.spawn(|| quick())
        Task.outcome(t)
    }

    describe("after", after)
}
